==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|enviruner}}, {{m|fro|environner||to surround}}, from {{m|fro|environ||around}}, from {{m|fro|en||in}} + {{m|fro|viron||a turn}}, from {{m|fro|virer||to turn, veer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɪnˈvaɪ.ɹən/|/ɛnˈvaɪ.ɹən/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪɹən|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|especially in plural}} A [[surrounding]] [[area]]
#* {{quote-book|year=1811|author=[[s:Author:Jane Austen|Jane Austen]]|title=[[s:Sense and Sensibility|Sense and Sensibility]]|section=[[s:Sense and Sensibility/Chapter 19|Chapter 19]]|passage=His spirits, during the last two or three days, though still very unequal, were greatly improved—he grew more and more partial to the house and '''environs'''—never spoke of going away without a sigh [...]}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[surround]]; to [[encircle]].
#* '''1673''', [[w:John Milton|John Milton]], “[[s:I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs|I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs]]”:
#*: I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs
#*: By the known rules of antient libertie,
#*: When strait a barbarous noise '''environs''' me
#*: Of Owles and Cuckoes, Asses, Apes and Doggs.
#* Spenser
#*: Dwelling in a pleasant glade, / With mountains round about '''environed'''.
#* Shakespeare
#*: '''Environed''' he was with many foes.
#* John Donne
#*: '''Environ''' me with darkness whilst I write.

